INT. FUNERAL PARLOR, SIDE ROOM - DAY

Mahogany. A low table of untouched cookies. Through the half-open door: murmurs, organ music, the shuffle of black shoes.

VIVIENNE ACHTERBERG-NOLAN (40s, black dress, hair coiled) pours tea with surgical care. Her brother ALISTAIR ACHTERBERG-NOLAN (40s, suit a size too small) paces. Their aunt, ROSALIND "ROZ" FENTON-ADEKUNLE (70s, cane, pearl choker), sits straight-backed on the sofa.

VIVIENNE
Everyone's here. Even the cousins from Perth.

ALISTAIR
That's a miracle.

ROZ
It's an inheritance.

VIVIENNE
Aunt Roz.

ROZ
I'm only saying what the room is thinking.

ALISTAIR
(muttering)
The room is thinking about sandwiches.

VIVIENNE
Nobody's thinking about sandwiches.

ALISTAIR
I am.

He takes a cookie. Bites. Chews with his back to them. Vivienne sets the teapot down. Her hands are steady, too steady.

VIVIENNE
Alistair.

ALISTAIR
Mm.

VIVIENNE
Did you read the will?

ROZ
^Did you read the will?

They both stop. Look at each other. Roz sips her tea.

ALISTAIR
Not yet.

VIVIENNE
Alistair.

ALISTAIR
I haven't had the heart.

ROZ
Then I'll give you the heart of it, dear. There isn't any money.

Silence. The organ rises through the wall.

VIVIENNE
What?

ROZ
Your father spent it years ago. On the house, on your schooling, on the lawyers. On me.

ALISTAIR
On you?

ROZ
I had a hip.

VIVIENNE
(very quietly)
He told us it was fine.

ROZ
He told everyone it was fine. It was his only vice.

Alistair sets down the cookie. Looks at his sister. Something passes between them, old and wordless.

ALISTAIR
(to himself)
Of course. Of course he did.

VIVIENNE
Alistair?

ALISTAIR
I'm going to go and thank everyone for coming.

VIVIENNE
Alone?

ALISTAIR
No. Come with me.

She nods. He offers his arm. She takes it.

ROZ
(calling after them)
Take a sandwich!

The door closes. Roz, alone, sets down her cup, and lets her shoulders drop for the first time.
